#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054682 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67e6a951-2645-5440-8059-14f0182933ff" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Few Terms for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "3c7dd3ad-eaa9-5312-adaf-3a0772e845f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-762" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the first three non-zero terms of the Taylor series expansion about $x=1$ of $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the first few terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty { {{n}}(x-1)^{n}} = (x-1) + {2(x-1)^2} + {3(x-1)^3} +\\cdots .\n$$\n\nTo find $f'(x),$ we differentiate term-by-term. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+0+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+4(x-1)+9(x-1)^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054582 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "cb7a106a-130a-5479-b60d-8ea76a8cd8de" :question/math-academy-id "e-763" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{ (x-3)^{n}}{n}\n$$\n\nFind $a_n,$ given that\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n![](images/73/7316ce9632bc2ab0347accb71c4c0dc90db8a87e0f9bead704e0218523a9b367.png)\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n![](images/84/846a66292603d3436219826be2e9419b5798fa121341d1e1676fba63c0f37eec.png)\nWe can now write the Taylor series for $f'(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\na_n = (x-3)^{n-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054583 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/id #uuid "e656ee8d-55b9-5ea6-beae-7c2caf329a6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-764" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$  about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x) = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-3)^{n}\\,x^n}{n^3}\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x| <            R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of $f'(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of $f'(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)^{n+1}\\,{x}^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{3}}{(-3)^{n}\\,{x}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-3)x\\cdot {n}^{3}}{(n+1)^{3}}| \\\\  & =3|x|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{3}} \\\\  & =3|x|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|x| & <1 \\\\ |x| & <\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence is $R = \\dfrac 1 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the a General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69ac432d-af48-5d58-9c4e-6b45fa9907cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ac432d-af48-5d58-9c4e-6b45fa9907cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319716} :answer-field/id #uuid "baaed7d0-ce21-5a59-8834-a8eeeb951918" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba1a2065-05f5-5d7a-9aeb-6e943be02b88" :question/math-academy-id "q-352520" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2x}{120}+⋯+{{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}}{(2n+1)!} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{(2n+1)!}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03fe077c-2f54-5d1e-9d94-e81bf9e7195c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03fe077c-2f54-5d1e-9d94-e81bf9e7195c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319405} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfd9f6f-00ee-5561-ae17-a34a75446cf8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd3fd116-3eb9-5711-87df-f837fd78e515" :question/math-academy-id "q-352603" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}.\n$$\n\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $|x|<R,$ where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ about $x=0.$\n\nThe radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The radius of convergence for the Taylor series of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is the same as the Taylor series of $f(x).$ So, to find the radius of convergence of ${f}^{′}(x),$ we need to compute $R.$\n\nTo calculate $R,$ we can use the ratio test. Computing the limit of the absolute value of the ratio of two consecutive terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(4(n+1)-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n+1}}{(4n-1){(\\frac{x}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{4n+3}{4n-1}||\\frac{x}{4}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|x|}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe series converges when $L<1.$ So, it converges when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|x|}{4} & <1 \\\\ |x| & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of convergence of the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is $R=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88b9751d-450e-5ae9-bda6-1c1d136bc929" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius of Convergence for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f7271c-5268-55b5-8703-f3cc507f5874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053386} {:answer/id #uuid "ed487fda-82b6-53a6-958c-56f559cf6e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053387} {:answer/id #uuid "35f7caa3-964e-5150-ab9e-6fc9acbcff36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053388} {:answer/id #uuid "5880b46d-d5c4-5958-8d55-183570fc1841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053389} {:answer/id #uuid "6bd68ceb-0543-55ca-92e6-971ba7ed329b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053390}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec3575f-fb34-5109-b19d-e6be26a7a0df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053385}] :question/id #uuid "1ca8106e-3840-584b-9cd7-ab688b28310f" :question/math-academy-id "q-50910" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x = 0$ is given by\n$f(x) = ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)((-1)^{n - 1})/(n!)x^{n}$.\nThe series converges to $f(x)$ for $\\mid x \\mid < R$, where $R$ is the radius of convergence of the Taylor series. Calculate the radius of convergence of the Taylor series expansion of $f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x)$, about $x = 0$."}]}
